outh-america", "targetName": "Spanish Exploration of South America", "context": "Niño's successful pearl trading voyage to the Venezuelan coast in 1499-1500 directly influenced subsequent Spanish exploration and exploitation of the region."}, {"sourceSlug": "age-of-discovery", "sourceName": "Age of Discovery", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "pedro-alonso-niño", "targetName": "Pedro Alonso Niño", "context": "Pedro Alonso Niño was a prominent navigator whose career and voyages were entirely encompassed within the pivotal Age of Discovery."}], "places": [{"name": "Palos de la Frontera, Spain", "role": "Departure point"}, {"name": "Paria Peninsula, Venezuela", "role": "Exploration site"}, {"name": "Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic", "role": "Colonial base"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Pedro Alonso Niño", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "pedro-alonso-niño"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q978906", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_Alonso_Ni%C3%B1o"], "tags": ["Explorers & Navigators", "cultural-diffusion", "environmental-history", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Pedro Alonso Niño's expert navigation was crucial to Columbus's initial success in 1492, directly facilitating the European discovery of the Americas. His independent 1499 voyage to the Pearl Coast of Venezuela solidified early Spanish claims and economic exploitation of South America, yielding significant wealth.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-18T16:19:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44343963456", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Pedro Alonso Niño was known in his time as Peralonso Niño, he was a Spanish navigator and discoverer. He piloted the Santa María during Christopher Columbus's first voyage to the Americas in 1492, and accompanied him on his second voyage in 1493.", "newValue": "Pedro Alonso Niño, often known as Peralonso Niño, was a pivotal Spanish navigator and explorer whose expertise in seamanship proved instrumental during the initial European encounters with the Americas. Born in Moguer, Palos de la Frontera, in the mid-15th century (exact date unknown), Niño emerged as a seasoned maritime figure during the burgeoning Age of Exploration, directly shaping the geographical understanding and colonial ambitions of the Spanish Crown between 1492 and his death in 1505.

Niño's most significant contribution began in August 1492, when he served as the master and pilot of the *Santa María*, the flagship of Christopher Columbus's first transatlantic expedition. His navigational acumen was critical in guiding the fleet across the Atlantic and safely returning the *Niña* to Palos de la Frontera in March 1493, following the shipwreck of the *Santa María*. He further participated in Columbus's second voyage to the Caribbean in 1493. In 1499, Niño commanded his own expedition, sailing to the Paria Peninsula (modern-day Venezuela), where he engaged in trade for pearls and gold, returning to Spain with considerable riches in early 1500.

